        <p>lle kept his valet with him antit a late hour, consulting with lino almut his plans in the most confidential manner, hot at litst ilimisaeil him. Then for hours Cony,. pared his room, the prey of atrangaly conflicting emotions. Ilk exultation M his auccesaful second wooing, in his growl prospects, in the favour of the rich old earl, in hie weldgreundeil expcatat ion of aucceeding to the flawlilmmt estates, varied iorangely with spawns of remorse for his base abandonment of his wile.</p>
        <p>Ile thought of her glorious I.iuty, her innoosnt faith and trust in him, her sweetnese, her spirit, her pride, the purity and strength of her nature, and compared her in hie own mind with the schaming, eelludi bairns, and a groan horst front hie lip., and he quickened his stile to an impetu. onii speed. ! " Why are thinzs never ita we want them ?" he muttered. "Poor Beryl ! If Alia liml only had birth and fortune Alter •Il 013 t has passed - notwithatanding that her twenty has nearly 10r,,! me to my ruin-1 love her still. I love her ! And I hate Slis• Windsor. What • grotesque mockery it ell is !"</p>
