        <p>Haggis a sincere desire 011 the pot of tier Majesty to be in mare immediate touch with her Minister., each reparation from Balmoral grown mare painfuLas the roll,bv. Every it t. of the Caret ..catil path among the hills, or tree within the drowse, reinind the Queen of once Albert, at whose in every detail of the !caste ma, planned MA faffif'd out. And another Wrong link betriiiin thi• Sovereign and her Scottish home is mado by the number of ••cairrin" (simple mound, of, stones—modern reproductli he it time ancient manner of marking a burial place, or the .t where • great battle had been fought ), which Weakly ell the ground. of Bali moral, and meet the eye at ever, turn. The first cairn built by the Queen wan raiseil in Oct°. her 1852, ti honour Hie porches, oi Balmoral. It is eight . feet in beteld, and perched in t tie highest peak of Craig !Gowan. Thin is called it, Queen's Cohn, and i,nnill occasionally visted by hr 11.ijisty, who obtains from beneath the ahadow if Is granite sides, a splendid view over the gardens, lawns, and tennis court., which sue. round the Castle. The marriages id all the Queen', children are commemorated loy cairns, each bearing an Inecrlption, and called by the WIMP of the Prince or Princess, to whom it is dedicated. Each of these cairns Is ten feet high, is built without cement, and shaped Irk« • C•Pr. A prettier, Cr smarter function than the marriage of the Marimens of Waterford to Lady Beatris Prances FP. Manrier, daughter of the Marquess of Marchnines. of Lanwireen,,hasnot boen vritrimen in London lon tome considerable time. The Prince of Wales was tl eeeee lookn lag very cheery and dlibunair, the bridesmaids were all young and esceptionally pretty, sail the bride • beau, full girl of eighteen, made a charming picture in her lovely whir gown, which glPtered with diamonds and silver. There wan • rumour to the effect that Empress Eugenie intended ta be mewed, hilt oinsidering the many year. that elapeed since Her Map sty QM 