        <p>Among godfathers, the P/iIICV of Wales is prnbsbly the record linlder. for hi. spon.ntship at the nftme•giving to the little son of the Duke. and Ductless of Marlborough, . rnunts fry the seventy-fifth occasion on which His Royal Highness has I,BlCiAtril in that cap ',city. Twentl-five years ago the fluke hirnself was tinnoured in • similar , fashion by the Prince. The email Marquess, whose robe by the way, was an exquisite creation, bet aced , beautifully. In the millinery wor.d things Russian are still • ing themselves wildly. Al the present moment it would he difficult Word to find a fashionable woman in either this country or Prance who is not possesd of • Russian blouse, or a Russian coat, for 011,10, wear. Anton. ran only marvel that this huld 13,3 so, for the mode has Isn't, to recommend it. .</p>
        <p>Coats built of plena velvet are considered very We have always been taught to believe, however, that the beauty of velvet lies in its soft pilo, sod velvet with the pile flattened has ever been c eindered a thing to be avoided. Hut new this beautiful fabric is to be reduced le • crinkled, crushed, and mutilated condition—it is then called phone velvet, and we are asked to admire it. Roe day wear the most up-to-date colour is Russian gre,n, not unlike watercreoi green,but for evening wear pink is in great request. ()owns ordered far forthcoming hunt and county MIN have much of this onlor scout them. And • blend ail pink and pale green bidet fair to enjoy consideraelo popularity • little later on in the eemlon.</p>
        <p>To those familiar with c; witty hfit, few exhibitions aro more iiitereating than the annual show of the British Dairy Farmers' Association. UM Bar ?dainty, the Queen, many of the mietocracy are dairy farmers and I the flithanne Burdett-Coutte. the Duchese of Newcastle, the I!. notes. of Letriledale, and many other dittinguiehed women an. among the exhilntora at this yemox show at the Agricultural Ball in London. On, of the most is. ferreting exhibits. is a newly-perfected butter-making I machine, by which the whole operation of producing butter from milk, is completed in the space of len than a minute. It is • ilimiliah invention and with this corn. parativele smell apparatus, one attendant can easily I produce 50 Or 60 pound. of butter, iu a Mort working day. It 0 commonly said that British dairying has fallen behind became the woman folk in the farm. have mostly abandoned the dairy for the piano, and other sewn. plishments, but the old 01;ject ion to the tedium and I. botious nature f the work 1141 longer holds Rood in the fan of them modern developments. These sew 111.1110 et are already in ate at 4001 Pol the Irish creameries, and ; when they aro mada in the miniature household size, it I will tie pomade in the country to have butter tor break. I feet msnufactured "white you watt." It., Ilaint,S dairy, to which only favoured visitor. are admitted, le a cbarsing place. A great cool hall, ; panelled with delicate-tinted tiles. At each end of the !dairy stand.. beautiful fountain, and round the walls are medallions of the Royal Family, with the monogram V.R.between,while the rsof is atippated by three arched pillowr. The dairy is lighted by stained xlms window. i• which the revs, WWI, and shatnrock, and primmer unite.and outside these ion Rte wire game to exclude any obetrusive insects. Our aristocracy appears to te gradually losing its traditional prejudice Aimee trade. It would be difficult to tell hors many in these (tactualre circles are, or have been, connected with bor.., liquor, or who have Me I tallied their present that 111Cli.gt toy mcCes. in one or ; other of the great induetries. Beside them we have had ; I noble coal merchant., cab owners, greangrucen,butehers, I milliners etc., several of whom have made no secret of their titled proprietorship. There is sow • noble lord who announce. Inca. of the Church papen that he Mil- lsupply, post free, chickens at ouch • price, md ducks. , rabbits, turkeys, grew, and soon. for so much. In order that the customers may conedenttally rely upon the geterneone of the tends' the addrees of his lordship'. I estate 4 ths•ght.f tally adds' to the advertisement.</p>
        <p>The great lumbering otate coach, need by the Lord Bloyor of London on the ecla of Noweint cr.ls no less than 140 Teary old, and nobody Isnot., hos much money has been altogether espeaded upon it, or to. rnuc.o of the otiginal "Luau. ',moist. This year it h. bean ',lush- , lobed ot • co. of £235, and It. orison,' coot was £1045 3.03. Loon after it was Ludt the oni,uol toll I poly. exceeded L. 300. ant it on cid bn priitty talc to soy that not le. than 025.000 h. been exprodert upon what might be *ma to he the CMon specimen of any haunts that is in AU 0000 outlay folio upon succeasion</p>
