      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1897-10-22_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>One rEstinguishedexplorer,Mr.Littledale,who a couple of years ago mile an effort to ernes thibet, and who, fortunately, maned the dreadful treatment to which Mr. Savage Landor area recently aubjected, has jat re- turned to England from Central Asia ; and another em- inent traveller, Mr. E. A. Vitagerald, is now on hi, stay hark from the Andre, South America. Both carry with them a wealth of material which will add largely in mane directions to our knowledge of regions so widely Apart a the continents of Asia and America. Myldttle• dale and his wife have been exploring Mongolia and the Altai tangent monntains,hut the expedition has been inn great measure sporting one,•ltheugh natural tory eame in for its due sham of interest. The ccllec- bon of specimens is rich in examples of wild sheep of • rare kintione of which wears horns 83 inches in length. Thin in un inclim longer than the horns of • sheep of the same specie. in the Kensington Museum, and, it is sani,Konsingtnn boats of the largest pair to he found in any museum in the world.</p>
        <p>The Belgians are preparing to keep the seventh cen- tenery of the diacovery of tel It. their territory. The I diacoverer was a blacksinith ot Liege, earned Hulliou— lhim, the French word °hotline" or coal. It appears that 111Atoriana are antiefied that HUNKn was an ntrr legendary person. Die forge greatly prospered owing to the coal he burned when all his rivals were burning wood. He became the great Walloon ironniaatar of hie I.e. 'The first coal iro was kindled by him in the smithy in 1197. I. 1223 his charcoal was burnt hr all the blacksmiths in Liege and Brabant. The French were not SIMA to the use of coal before the fourteenth cen- tury, but the Duke. of Burgundy were. The collieries of Anzio, however, were not discovered before 1734. lie discover is burial in the church nf Ladinsart,in the Fits. de-Calais. His tombatone tastillen that he tank the first pit at Amain.</p>
        <p>As Japan is adopting so many features of European civilisation and development we need hardly be sur- prised that labour questions are cropping up with bimo Iri ipiency there. The British Consul at Nagasaki apes in him latest report that ''the docility, patience, and subordination formerly so characteristic of the Japanese workman are now things of the past." Strikes, once unknown, are now by no means uncommon." and they ars accomponied by the wont features of tbae in Eurorie—tolcotting, picketing, and assaults on black- legs."</p>
