      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1897-10-22_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>OCTOBER. 22nd 1E197</p>
        <p>One embjeet was dissuosl at a laduersi Educstien. meeting of the Mancheeter City Council on Wednoldey which is of intereit to many people betide* the residents of Manchester. This was the subject of technical education on the I loritinont, especially no boarlfig upon English industries. A &amp;potation eppointed by the Technical beitniction Committee of the kfanchenter Corporation has recently visited the technical echools, institutions and museums in liermany and Austria, and their report upon whet they have then, seen in evidently an interesting document. 'lle deputation has been very deeply impressed by the elf ins that have been made ~road, and especially in lerinany, to develope technical instruction. The advances that have been made in recent years by our German com- petitors have, it wontl appear, been systematic and ontilinous, and thorn miens to be no inten- tion on thu part of these persistent and wide- awake foreigners ti relax their energies until they have renclied the very fr int rank of nide.- trial nations. This Manchester deplitation ern- phaaises the old lesson that the pressure of foreign competition] is, in its not vital annO., only another phase of the great eltimit /motion. 10 this country we hare anon within the last two or three years • pswerful 1: ininituent dealing with the schonls of the country WI thigh education was purely, or, at all oV•o, tn. primarily a uutional question. The trinni ohjoef. of oor Txry rulers has been the streaft honing of denoininid- ism and not the perfecting of the educations/ system with a view to aim commercial end oohs- , ' trial welfare in the Intimo. This is not the way • the educational marlunery is managed in many. Too Manchester &amp;mutation gives lull credit to what is described a. the " freedom, variety and elasticity of our English eineatioriel system, lintel the same tine, it declares that it ix high time "the effort was intl.lo .1 this unintry to give to ow youth the slimational advantages,