        <p>EXTRAORDINARY STORY 01 MARRIED</p>
        <p>LIFE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Pollee Court on Monday,hefore Megan, , P. I. Norman and S. Taylor, William Worrell was rum- ' moiled at the instance of his wife, Margaret Wormll,re- siding in Ilalton.road, Runcorn, far petsistent cruelty. M.. Werrall acid her husband (the defendant I had been living away from her for ~a isiontlis. lie had burnt her clothe., set her bed on fire, and had annoyed her when ha met her in the etreet. He had repeatedly broken the window, in her house. She had had to have bag% over the window. on Sundays. Defendant had broken pounds worth of things. On the last visit he made to her howe he cams with • penknife and very nearly cut witnem'a haulm". Mia war,, August. In reply to the MegintrateXClerk,the witness explained that on the occasion referred to, the defendant " sneaked " into the house in ho slippers and got hold other, and torah, r drewr oft her hack. Dolendant hind broken her windows twice. Ile had said that he wanted to cot her heal off. fie had 111-treated her an several occasions. lie had turned her out at at hours, and she hod been ob- liged to sloe. anywhere. They had been illifried ar,out 30 year, When defendant met his awn child in the street he would not acknowled, Crose.examined by Mr.J.W. Knowlaeorho represented the defendant, witileri nail she could not say on ash it day she turned her hionand out awl put his box astnide. About March or April the deten.lant had been drinking for some days, and witnens packed up hit clothe*, and put hi. loos (anal In and locked the door, tie broke the window. in trying to get in the triune. From that day till now they had not lived together. Mr. Knowles pointed out that the nommen% charged the defendant with pereiatent cruelty whereby he liii ceased the complainant to I'are him and live sew Wel, apart from him. , Complainant, in reply to tuether questions denied that die had expresses' regret that tho defendant wan not dead. '</p>
        <p>Mr. Knowles said defendant was turned out by com-1 plainant and her aon. Be auggreted that if the psalm could only he persuaded by the P.liiCe Court Minionary, I or snmerniniater of rellgion,to make it up, he thought It would be • good thing. The defendant was in a weak state of health and he was willing to go hack to the house. He intimated that he would consent to a primaratl,in without an order for malntainenee. 'rhe Bench granted • separation order without main- Wiwi, and the Chairman auggetted that they slimild try and become reconciled.</p>
