        <p>' The jury having been solemnly Morn in the High Bailiff made the ainial proclamation. , Mr. Dunn. raid it warned to tome people a sort of • farce to hold that court. Rut the fact nf the matter was that they had held records selfish he (Hr. Huhu) ear.. liinnell to Sir Thomas Hardy at the Record Offlce,datins ae far hack an 1220.arah the names of the Jury appended to them. The fuel i inn of those court. hat now almost ceased. In the put there were no c ,urte except theae local courts, beyond the assizes, whir.] were held twiu • year, and in those day s tho ju Igoe ride n horseback. The Court Leet then settled all private dieputes in the rnenare and places where they were held, lip the County Court Act and many other Acts which were palmed a great deal of the waft they did was now done elsewhere and by other bodies. The superviainn of the roads and lanes, drainage and nuisances, and things which would naturally present themselves a• mioinng smendrne9t by the people living in these manors and places were formerly settled by • jury nf neighbours, and in thou day. they were very useful indeed. It wee true that these Dula. ceased to have any real putpoge or func- tion., hut at the game time, when he was spiointed by the Queen under the anal which they had an..,, they compelled him to hold these courts as part ef the coeds Son 01 his taking the appointment. Those who had come there to meet him knew what • great Ve Eturn it hal been te keep un • custom which had dated from 1220; and how much further back they amid not tell. As long as he was epued tm treaded to hold these gouts, and ag mg as they wnuld favour him with their presence it iv ald ertainly he • pleasure to him, and ening for them to reflect upon many year. after his $ had been filled. Last you he was not able to be with them, hut his partner Mr Kirkconnel kindly took hie place. It. had been better this year, and was pleased to he able to meet them again. (Hear, hear., Some ad• yew cri.irlsrne had hewn