        <p>The Bev. W. H. Daransomu, In Cairo-street Chapel, on Sunday ramble, give his third sermon on the cardinal Virtue, his subject being .• Temperance." The preacher took for hie text the words •• Every man that striveth for the mastery is temperate in all things,. and If The truit of the spirit is temperance." Temperance, or self- contr,,,, said the pro ache, was one of the causes of the greater. of Greek life, and also the greatness of that wonderful legacy of art whira wen left to he the wonder of all future age, hut when that great view of self- control began to die away throb art been to decline and the people became the violins of those who were igroncer sral in to temperate than themsolvcs Tomper- ence Mee Dot so trinch from the circuratiturans in which men were c ravelled to lice.. from qualities, desires, lust:nets, and prasioni of 15040 own nature. What was omit, raf il he was not to be the slave of his circum- stance, if he seas not to yield to the baser instincts of his nature 1 Here he was finding the path of virtue a difficult one to tread, with many endued°. nn every Side to draw him away It un the path of temperance. There 'were with,, hnn strong untrained impulnes. The plezeures nftlo, ea, hearing, and taste must all be Weed temperately. Pram most always remain the master of them, and never allow himself to become the bond slave of the none.. 'Mere wen, ocraeions when to a man the only Umperencs wee rigid rastineece, but it might sot be so for his ninglitiour. The frol gifts of God were to be accepted with thankfulnese. lin did not see why ....scent that it did tint At in quite with the co nirential language ot devotion—t ioy shoind not thank find in their prayers for their merry-makings, their happy family getheringhfor i heir concerts, parties and dances, because If these things were right for them at all, ache believed the, were, then they should link them with the thought of fil.d and ot rfligion, and they should have eneman enough simply to thank God for them good gifts. But they must not forget that all these things bad their dangers if used in elem. Tbere were some who had a great love for pleasuee In their nature. It a; rang from an exuberance of vitslity, run- nel spirits. riod had made them that way. sue He ezpected that they, as His childron, should turn even the love of pleasure,this gaiety of pleasure, to the use of His kingdom and His glory, and they must ever gernind thenwelves and other, that the condition of their Using their pleasured rightly was that they should ...m- -elee temperance. Allusien was made by the preacher to Intemperate spending of money, overdreasing, lavish Sod unscrupoloue expenditure with regard to f urnishing a housemiii he said it would he well for them to remem• bee the wise e ords of a good Enallehmse who died not Very long ego,that the true style of • gentleman was to be rather inclifferpot to outward thing.. rho body and Ct. app' firm is must be kept in .objection. They must sten he temperate in judgment and In speech. How many good CIMPII9.SoiI even religion Itself, suffered from Intemperate speech ! They would not misunderstand birn if he rail—lie his heart was in the cauve—that he believed the rause nt temp vance—ueing the word In the harrow meow in winch it was often employed—hid suf- fered not • little in influenre, in the power of m oldln the lives of men wing to the intemperate speech and the Intemperate j.i.franent of its advocates. The more they believed in a thing he more they realised It. value, not naly tor their own lives but the souls of other men, and the mote should they be careful to present it to ether. in form winch would captivate them motet, not in anger, net in bitterness, not even in on. bridled enthusiasm, toit with .4.1 f-control with temper- ratco,belleving that troth sod goodness had force enough Is themselves without any adventitious aid from them.</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. WYCLIFFE P.B.A. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>'There was agnnslnrgs ;Weil-dance et the meeting of Usk society eunday afternoon. The chair was occu- pied by the WS li. 0. Whitfield, of Oyer, who, In the course ot a shert addreas, referred to the character of Christ, and urged hi hearers to try and follow in His Steps. Mr ikorgr Power, of Manchester, delivered the address for the afternoon. Ile spoke on tt I.nve," and said that when men came to realise that love was the embodiment of Christ's Ike tbere would be better feeling between man and ;non. He referred to the ettilies and conflicts between employer and employed, and to the wretcbcdnets and -walnue in our midst, due to the sel- fishness and indiff -refine of man to man. As illuetrating . bit remarks, Mr. Pnorr spoke of the condition of entre of the people in Manchester, Riving Instances which had come under him own observation. The musical portion of the service was rendered additionally attractive by an excellent performance of two instrumental trios by Mews. H. Hotel, H. H. Pearson. andW.H.Yaytrin on the violoncello. vi din and nce an respectively.— The nervier vu also taken part in by MeTnomaa Walllngtovide W. ikawick, and Mr. W. Meow.</p>
