        <p>STOCKTON IIIsATII. Hir,--'l'here are two rT three points In Mt. Bouchier's 1 ter that I should like to comment upon. Reference is ' ads ha his letter to an "agreement" whereby Mr. Was. i don ill said to give up • certain strip of land lad Sri. act that the authorities had a right to claim that amount 1 (land even if Mr. Warburton hint purchased it ? If so , Council has gained nothing. The monetary value ol whole lam', Mr. 'touchier any., in small, yet it is no (liable to Mr. Wart talon that Mr. 'touchier believes would fight to the utmnat to retain rewsesaion. 'I hie, I take it, ph ,we that Mr. Warburton 's a good judge nt land, lot ouch • mite with no magnificent a frontage is I certainly worth fighting for. "We might lose," fear. I fully says Meßnucluer, "and have the costs to pay." To ! talk of losing in lace ol an offer once made to ren.ore the ' land (in its 'original rundition)atglivs • faint-heartedness which I could hardly have thought pomible in • perish councillor—at an eh Minn. A councillor worth his Salt does not fight his battles lying down. Everything is in favour of the Council's claim to the land, not excepting the Highway Board incident,yet the enuncillom--eacept three or four—me consumed by • ridiculous tread of • litigation hogey,or else regard the restoration of the land air trifling compared with the alteration of • defective drain or an (.d.difer us midden. Now, if the Mighway Beard made a present of the n(1 to Mr. Warburton the claim of the Parinn would he serioualy prejudiced. But the preliumPtion is that the Highway lt,erd Bar done nothing of the kind. The matter can easily he pettiest by a referenre to the words. Therefore let Mr. /touchier produce them.—Yours, ho., CONSERVATOR.</p>
        <p>THE ENCROACHMENT MCESTION. linuchier'a letter which appeared in your Isaac f Hat Saturday cammanda, I think it will to. con- ceded, • reply from me. I regret that • should at the nutaet charge me with not bring as he Hate) "as suffi- ciently exact and frank in nip statement at the int.,' view as throe V. know me vv.- uld have expected." Unlnttunately,h,,revir,Mr. touchier falls headlong into the very error he was under the impression I had cons- mitted. Strange fatality. yw example. He aaya that I might have stated that • District Councillor informed us that the High- way B wral Kato Mr. Warburton this land about 2.5 years ago, Ike." The words uttered were I believe mem- bers .d the Highway Board gave the land, ho. "hut as I do not wish to appear to split hairs on the subject I will at the moment accept the statement so Mr. !touchier mentions it. Now what I Irish to knnw ig, why did not Mr. }touchier complete this stnry about the If iglivray Ward and give us the while facts Hating to it P Why did he leave out the vital part connecred with it ! ha has not done ant willingly fill in the gap, and I trust Mr. liqUelliPr will pardon me for doing so It is however so important that I must not under the circumstances keep it hack. The gap H this : "N.. recnrd can he found or traced lathe Minute Books of the Highway Board of PUV.II • traosasti m ever hating taken place. The Clork un the Council has made a special search and fail, to find out ans minute or document relating to the subject." You will see therefore that the stevnwn: is valueless and therefore not permissible as</p>
        <p>evidence. In pitman., allow me to say that it would he N.M..' if members of the Parish Council would leave Mr. War- burton hi weave his own case and not supply him with threads to make one. Now the chief queatlon affecting hie land is • Ilas Mr. Warburton any intl." to this and. The answer Is thiA 18 that hit remaining in pet. session for 23 years has not glees him •t. title. llt. Law does not recognise tints pnwerielon fur title iota, as common lands " are concerned. The late pariah Council must have limn satianed as to this, otherwise they would not have ',quested Mr. Warburton to give gp possemion. Now the only questions the parishioners need concern themselves about are—On they with to have the names- shun of the whole of the land I or, are they willing that the suggested give and take line agn ement ails tine 6 feet shall he carried into effect 1 Do the latter point the parishioners should clearly 1111- dentand whether Mr. Warburton remain. in poste.ion of the land, or whether it a taken rq fl tht leo., him, Clint a portion of it will have to be given up for footpath purpose.. The County Council have already claimed eit. an We land side frorn the hedge for that very purpose. The late Pariah Council have thereforr gained little or no land 1.4 the Par sit. and !for erne •m aaton• 'shed that an endesesUr abould for one moment lie trade to foist swat an arrangement epee the Parish and then •re the stock phrase ..It is in the interests 1 the</p>
        <p>rethlo I esn only excuse itter.f the members of the Nte • Parish Counril and th.dn of the present one in theirs).. tinn that they do not sppear to know °That • portion of this land would have to be given up for footpath mu, poops.° But even if it had not been so the arrangement would. I contend, he abaurd. fir • thouliand yards of i Vahiable front Wilding land ion 120 ponds ' It the I ides is peepoeterolle Mr. !touchier .111 I hope, pardon me if I again refer to him, but I only do to ae he tried to raise the fears of the pealshiomm no to henvy law coat., k We might be Involved in serintut litigation and to ,111 he says I Moiled when I read this. WV/eVer. Ido not blame him lon doing schfor unfoitunately this idea is only too preva- lent aki it therefore will be emit to inform the public as to what the procedure will he M recover the land. II the parishioners state that it is their wish to have this land Mr. Warburton will be called upon to 11,111.ve the fence and should hereto. then the District Surveyor will be instructcd to knock it down [hat is all the legal perinea that is needed. LA, me my in ronclusion,that I have nothing to gain in ails/orating that the people .11-iuld meek to regain that which is their own. I impute no unworthy motives to those who differ with me on this ipmetlon. It is for the people theimelvea t decide and to say whether they are prepared to sell 000 f their birthrights for what seem. M me less than a mete ° mew of pottake."Let the people , visit the spot and use the judgment with which they are each endowed. 'llls °3" MI whom r trlm pia,a.4 to form i.itao have toren ootyrded r it this queetionfonly four Stockton Weill representatives awhilrt iv, and •II that Wean now dolt to put the people on thir guard and oars them that If they wish to prevent what appear. to us to to. an arrangement of little value, and a gift of one r f tho finest building sites in Stockton Heath, they must call for • paridi meeting end pass• resolution that